erge peaks while peak counts match (perfect-tree sizes equal): new_hash = H(0x01 || right_peak || new_node). careful with ordering. Standard MMR append: peaks are ordered largest→smallest by subtree height; when appending a new leaf, repeatedly take the last peak (smallest) and combine: node = H(0x01 || peak || node)? Convention (CT/Trillian-style RFC 6962 uses different structure; MMR as in Peter Todd's opentimestamps): peaks sorted by height descending; append: while there exist ≥2 peaks with equal height h, pop both a,b and push H(0x01 || a || b). Then push new leaf. Root = fold right-to-left: acc = last; for remaining peaks p (descending height): acc = H(0x01 || p || acc). That's the "bagging" step. I'll specify exactly this, with domain-separated prefixes LEAF=0x00, NODE=0x01 to prevent second-preimage across levels. Update S: head_root = bagged root, head_seq++, peaks/node_count updated, write blob+commit, fast-forward ref. Emit receipt (root, seq) — this is what gets cross-published on the bus/gossip channel for witnesses. Genesis: empty log root defined as SHA-256 of zero bytes? Cleaner: define EMPTY_ROOT = SHA-256("") = e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855. Genesis entry seq=0 has prev_root = EMPTY_ROOT. Good, exact constant. **Byte-identity acceptance test**: The key phrase "proves byte-identity. " Interpretation: a verifier who receives the ledger branch over the git bus must be able to prove that the byte stream they hold is byte-for-byte identical to what the writer serialized — i. , deterministic re-derivation: recompute every canonical entry from the received objects? No wait — the entries ARE the bytes; you can't re-derive payload contents independently unless the test includes known inputs. So byte-identity proof has these layers: (a) **Chain verification**: For every entry i: parse bytes, check magic/version/lengths, check seq == i, check prev_root_i == root_{i-1} recomputed locally, recompute leaf hash and rebuild MMR incrementally, assert final root equals published head root. Any single flipped bit anywhere breaks either length/format checks, hash equality, or the final root. This proves the received bytes hash-commit exactly to the published roots. (b) **Determinism/replay test (writer-side)**: Given the same sequence of logical appends (inputs), run writer twice in fresh temp dirs → the two bare repos must be byte-identical: compare `git rev-parse refs/heads/ledger`, and deeper: pack both repos (`git pack-objects` / repack with fixed params) and compare resulting . pack digests; also compare every object id list. Because commit metadata is pinned (fixed ident, dates taken from entry timestamp, deterministic message), object construction is pure. This proves byte-identity at the git-bus layer. (c) **Round-trip test**: reader clones/fetches the branch, extracts blobs, concatenates canonical entries in seq order into `log. bin`, and asserts sha256(log. bin) equals the value recorded by the writer sidecar receipt; and asserts per-entry equality against a golden fixture generated by the writer. Byte-identity of extracted stream vs original stream. I should write the actual test code — concrete. Language: pick one, say Python 3. 11+ stdlib + subprocess git, since it's unambiguous and runnable. Provide full code: canonical serializer/parser, MMR, writer, verifier, and pytest acceptance tests including the adversarial bit-flip cases (tamper matrix: flip byte in payload → expect failure at entry k root mismatch; truncate tail → expect head-root mismatch; reorder two entries → seq check fails; swap payload between two entries → both leaf hashes mismatch). Also need exactness on the git plumbing commands used by the writer to avoid environment-dependent bytes: - `git init --bare --initial-branch=ledger` (or set HEAD) - create blob: `git hash-object -w --stdin < entry_bytes` → but better do it programmatically with `git hash-object -w --path`? Path affects nothing for blob id (blob id is 